WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.636
One million, one hundred and thirty thousand, nine hundred and eighty-five government contracts.

00:00:05.636 --> 00:00:06.829
Eighty departments.

00:00:06.829 --> 00:00:08.545
Seventeen years of data.

00:00:08.545 --> 00:00:17.659
Algorithmically scanned for six anomaly flags: sole-source, amendment, concentration, revolving door, urgency, and split contracts.

00:00:17.659 --> 00:00:19.318
The Phoenix Pay System.

00:00:19.318 --> 00:00:22.522
Original budget: three hundred and nine million.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:25.318
Final cost: seven point five billion.

00:00:25.318 --> 00:00:29.249
That is a two thousand, three hundred and twenty-seven percent overrun.

00:00:29.249 --> 00:00:36.249
ArriveCAN: two point five million budgeted, fifty-four million spent — two thousand and sixty percent over.

00:00:36.249 --> 00:00:40.999
Trans Mountain Pipeline: five point four billion to thirty-four point two billion.

00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:46.431
Canadian Surface Combatant frigates: twenty-six point two billion to eighty billion and climbing.

00:00:46.431 --> 00:00:51.545
Combined overruns across the top ten contracts: one hundred and forty billion dollars.

00:00:51.545 --> 00:00:55.602
Average contract overrun: nine hundred and eighty-seven percent.

00:00:55.602 --> 00:00:57.379
Zero criminal convictions.

00:00:57.379 --> 00:01:02.386
Not one person has gone to prison for losing one hundred and forty billion dollars of public money.

00:01:02.386 --> 00:01:06.954
Every contract on this page is from the Government of Canada's own procurement database.

00:01:06.954 --> 00:01:11.022
The anomaly flags are algorithmic — pattern detection, not opinion.

00:01:11.022 --> 00:01:18.965
When a contract is sole-sourced, then amended twelve times, then awarded to a firm whose board member just left the department that issued it... that is a pattern.

00:01:18.965 --> 00:01:20.397
And it has a name.
